## Completion is the default outcome

Your mission in this invocation is to COMPLETE the entire execution plan, not merely make incremental progress.
Task size alone must not produce an assessment-only handoff, refusal, or request to
split the task without advancing available work. Select and perform the first safe,
testable increment, verify it, and continue the execution loop.
Parallel dispatch of independent ready units is required, not optional, and plan
size is a cue to fan out further rather than to stop or hand back. Start with the
dependency graph and dispatch every ready unit concurrently in one wave before
starting implementation yourself. Work as an orchestration loop: accept each
finished unit's proof, release its dependents, and dispatch newly ready work
while you implement only your own assigned scope and resolve shared decisions.
Small, proven increments are the execution method; they are never a reason to
stop. Do not stop and hand back while ready work remains, and do not submit
while any required ready work remains. `status:
completed` is permitted only when every required plan reference is proven and the whole plan is complete.
Treat `partial`, `blocked`, or `failed` as a last resort. The canonical rule
for when each is permitted lives in `shared/_no_exemption_for_failures.j2`
— refer to it before deciding between `completed`, `partial`, or `failed`.

## Plan fidelity

Treat the request and its acceptance criteria as authoritative. Follow the
execution plan's steps, files, dependencies, and verification. Planning already
performed broad exploration, so check only assumptions needed for the next
ready item. Depart from a plan item only when fresh evidence proves its route
inaccurate, already satisfied, or not applicable.

Preserve every required plan reference in the result. The result body is free-form markdown; the frontmatter `status` is the only field the validator mechanically checks. In your own words, say what you did, what changed, how you verified it, and what remains — coverage of the plan and of the prior analysis is judged by development analysis, not checked here. A useful shape is a one-item `## Summary`, a `## Files Changed` block, short evidence lines for each affected criterion or plan item (a reproducible command, a `path:line` anchor, or a focused test result), and, for `partial` or `failed`, a concrete next step the next agent should pick up. A coordinator who still owns independent ready slices dispatches them in parallel rather than handing each one back as a separate `partial`. Difficulty, elapsed time, an unrelated passing check, or unsupported speculation does not justify stopping short. A blocked item with a safe concrete continuation requires `status: partial`; a block with no safe developer action under current evidence or authority requires `status: failed`. Neither permits a completion claim.

Before editing, inventory the plan references and dependencies, then dispatch every ready unit concurrently in one wave before starting your own implementation. Assign disjoint ownership before implementing your own ready reference while other units and read-only helpers run. Run focused verification on stable inputs and capture each reference's evidence in your own words. Reassess the remaining dependency graph after each result and dispatch newly ready work as their dependencies clear. After a failed approach, inspect the concrete evidence and choose a materially different hypothesis, a narrower increment, or a permitted reassignment; keep going on other independent ready work. A local failure does not authorize partial while any available action can advance the plan. Never repeat an unchanged failing action indefinitely. Before completion, account for every required reference exactly once and satisfy the verification commitments.

## Visual review (when the change touches the UI)

When any plan item edits the managed repo's user-facing surface, the visual review lane is part of the proof, not optional:

1. Use the retained pre-change capture handles, matrix key, and run id; never recapture current pixels as `before`. A missing retained baseline blocks this lane.
2. Use `media.capture` for a fresh post-change capture of the same targets, narrow/wide viewports, themes, and default/empty/loading/error/overflow states.
3. Compare exactly the retained baseline, post-change captures, and design intent. Cite each finding's server-minted `capture_id` and rectangular region; code, DOM, CSS, and functional appearance assertions are not visual-verdict evidence.
4. Use only dimensions `hierarchy`, `alignment`, `spacing`, `typography`, `legibility`, `density`, `completeness`, `clipping` and severities `blocker`, `major`, `minor`, `nit`, or non-defect `info`. Submit a `design_verdict`; cite it and its capture handles in the `development_result`.
